=== (1 + 2) * 3 ===
  BFS order:       * + 3 1 2
  DFS pre-order:   * + 1 2 3
  DFS post-order:  1 2 + 3 *

Why post-order works for evaluation:
  Post-order visits '1' then '2' then '+', so when we
  process '+' both operand values are already known.

Why BFS does NOT work for evaluation:
  BFS visits '*' before '+' (at level 0 before level 1),
  but to evaluate '*' we need the VALUE of '+' first.

Evaluated result: 9.0  (expected 9.0)
